{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested on exact head fdb4919eca7b21402b3062a9c93867c64e1666f7, request/mcp-round-trips, against artifacts 190c56e5 (cmd/gitseq-mcp), f0762003 (internal/statusview), e421412a (internal/service), 07d4bd05 (docs).\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers from docs/reference/architecture.md at this head: layer 6, Projections and queries (internal/statusview: the new artifacts.go exact-path live-artifact page, the WorkDetails and WorkReview row enrichment, and fillCommitmentText replaced by fillCommitmentDetails over one shared enrichWorkRows pass) and layer 7, CLI, MCP, skills, connectors, and UI (internal/service: the new POST /v0/artifact-query route; cmd/gitseq-mcp: the new artifacts tool, laneResponseLimit, and projectionNotes now always emitting the fold verdict). Both layer contracts change, and docs/reference/architecture.md is updated in this same head in its layer 6 section, with the docs candidate artifact 07d4bd05 published at this exact head. That satisfies the rule that a contract change updates the page in the same head and publishes its candidate there. Layers 1 to 5 are untouched: no kernel, nexus, host-binding, or interpreter file appears in the diff, and no fold authority moves. projectionNotes still only reports decisions the fold already made, read from a workspace snapshot taken after the record landed, so the write surfaces disclose the ruling rather than predicting it. Architecture contract preserved except where documented.\n\nSECURITY. The bounds bind, and I found no defect in them. normalizeArtifactQuery refuses an empty paths list, caps the list at ArtifactPathMax of 20 before deduplication, refuses an empty path string, defaults limit to 20, and refuses any limit outside 1 to 50. The pagination arithmetic is correct on both edges: position is the zero-based index among matching rows, a row is appended only while position is at or above offset and len(rows) is below limit, end is offset plus len(rows), Remaining is matching minus end, and NextCursor is emitted only when end is below matching. An offset past the end is refused rather than silently clamped. Cursors convey no authority: the path set comes from the request and the cursor need only agree with its sha256 digest, so a forged cursor cannot widen a query to paths the caller did not ask for; a negative offset, a head that does not match, and a filter that does not match are each refused. Path strings are used only as exact map keys, with no cleaning, joining, globbing, or filesystem access, so they carry no injection surface. Request size stays bounded by the existing http.MaxBytesReader of 2 MiB with DisallowUnknownFields and guardMutation on the new POST route, so oversized path strings cannot exhaust the resident. laneResponseLimit is overflow-safe: the guard comparing ceiling against (maximum minus base) divided by rows clamps before the multiply, base is far below the 64 MiB adapter ceiling in every call site, and the result can never exceed that ceiling. The local fallback runs the same BuildArtifactPage over a verified workspace snapshot and marks the answer degraded. No trust or authority boundary widens.\n\nOne security-adjacent defect remains, and it is disclosure rather than authority. In internal/statusview/query.go, WorkReview copies Report, Verdict, and Ratified out of workroom.Review and drops Retired and Stale, which the fold sets in the very same struct literal that fills Ratified (internal/workroom/fold.go, in the review append near line 1504). enrichWorkRows then chooses the latest review for a head by last-match-wins over projection.Reviews, without skipping retired ones. A status, work, or wait row can therefore read latest_review with verdict approved and ratified true for an approval that cmd/gs refuses outright: standingStatement returns \"statement is retired\", and liveStatement additionally returns \"statement describes a superseded world\". This is not hypothetical at the current frontier. The live log holds 357 reviews, of which 11 are retired and approved and 156 are stale and approved. The stated purpose of this change is that routine triage no longer needs one inspect call per row; for the single fact that decides whether a head can merge, the caller must still inspect, and a caller who trusts the row is told the opposite of what merge will do. The right shape is already established one file away in this same head: ArtifactRow carries Retired explicitly even though it is always false for a returned row, and docs/reference/mcp/artifacts.md argues for exactly that, saying carrying it explicitly prevents callers from having to infer that fact from an omitted field. The request conditions name stale, retired, and world-stale flags as a review item, and this head applies that reasoning to the artifact query but not to the review row.\n\nRequested change, small and additive, with no weakening of any condition of satisfaction:\n1. Add Retired and Stale to WorkReview, non-omitempty for the reason already written above Ratified: a caller must be able to tell a withdrawn verdict from a row with no review evidence.\n2. Make the latest-review selection say what it means about retired verdicts, either by skipping them when choosing the latest or by exposing the flag so the caller decides. As written, a retired later verdict silently shadows an earlier live one at the same head.\n3. Add the two fields to the row table in docs/reference/mcp/work.md.\n4. Extend TestStatusAndWorkRowsCarryActionableTriageFields with a retired approved review, so the behaviour is held by a test rather than by prose.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The shape is close to minimal and I found nothing to cut without weakening the conditions. Dropping statementIndex from BuildWorkPage in favour of the shared enrichWorkRows is a real reduction: the bounded page no longer allocates a projection-sized map merely to label at most fifty rows, and one helper now serves BuildWorkPage, BuildActorStatus, and BuildWait where two stood before. Two observations, neither of them a request. First, enrichWorkRows makes three full passes over Statements, Artifacts, and Reviews and gives up the early exit that fillCommitmentText had; at this depth that is immaterial, and the multi-pass structure is what let the duplicate index go, so I would keep it as written. Second, ArtifactRow.Retired is always false by construction, but the page documents why it is carried anyway and that reasoning holds, so it should stay.\n\nTWO NON-BLOCKING OBSERVATIONS. The new page docs/reference/mcp/artifacts.md names db794e0d in rests_on, the cmd/gitseq-mcp artifact that was already retired before this head was written, while its live successor 3e42417e at cmd/gitseq-mcp for merged main 84de35f6 was available. internal/docset classifies that as a report rather than a fatal citation, because the retirement names a covering successor, and I confirmed empirically that the docset gate is green; but a brand new page starts life in the re-anchor queue for no reason, and the fix is one line. The other citation, 9627cf18 at internal/statusview, is live and current. Separately, the architecture.md edit leaves a ragged line wrap around the sentence beginning \"internal/app opens a repository\"; cosmetic only.\n\nGATES. Every gate below was run by me in a detached worktree at the exact head fdb4919eca7b21402b3062a9c93867c64e1666f7, not taken from the implementation report. go build ./... exit 0. go vet ./... exit 0. gofmt -l . empty. git diff --check 84de35f6..HEAD exit 0. go test -count=1 ./... exit 0, with all 25 packages ok, including internal/docset at 79.4s, internal/statusview at 4.7s, internal/service at 45.5s, and cmd/gitseq-mcp at 84.4s. go test -count=1 -race ./internal/statusview ./internal/service ./cmd/gitseq-mcp exit 0. No ui/ file is touched, so no UI gate applies. Rebase verified independently: git merge-base main fdb4919e returns 84de35f6c048e156937df10d380874cb7180ee86, which is both main and origin/main, so the rebase is clean and the branch is one commit ahead. origin/request/mcp-round-trips is at fdb4919e. Before promising this review I checked all four artifacts: each is live and not retired, each records commit fdb4919e, and none carries describes_superseded_world; the ordinary staleness on the governing chain does not block review or merge.\n\nCONTRACTS THE FOLLOWING SERIALIZED CHANGE MUST RESPECT. This head is the agreed serialization point ahead of the staleness-presentation work, and it fixes five things that later change should build on rather than duplicate. First, latest_review is now the name and object shape by which a row reports review state, carrying report, verdict, and a deliberately non-omitempty ratified; the two missing flags above are the natural place for that work to land if this lane declines to take them. Second, the artifact row settles the flag vocabulary as stale, retired, and describes_superseded_world, all explicit and never inferred from an omitted field. Third, projected.verdict is now always present on a successful state, ratify, or supersede result, including the value effective, so nothing downstream may read an absent verdict as an absent ruling. Fourth, the artifacts tool and the /v0/artifact-query endpoint are now the bounded way to ask which artifacts are live at an exact path, and any staleness presentation should read that rather than downloading the whole status. Fifth, laneResponseLimit has already spent part of the adapter byte ceiling on per-row conditions, so further per-row fields consume that headroom and should be weighed against it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:190c56e503e3d39c728c431c576f0c7864cc2e4f","head":"fdb4919eca7b21402b3062a9c93867c64e1666f7","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:120642db50483bb2207b41eb3abc0a0612dde04d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3